 "notes": "ODWC \"Results from 2025 Controlled Hunts\" (posted 2026-04; same table as the PDF https://www.wildlifedepartment.com/sites/default/files/2026-04/2025%20Controlled%20Hunts%20Results.pdf and the \"2025-26 Controlled Hunt Results\" table on https://www.wildlifedepartment.com/hunting/controlledhunts). CONTROLLED HUNTS ONLY, reported per hunt AREA and method (A/M/G), not per hunt number. hunters = \"# Hunters\"; harvest = Antlered + Antlerless; successPct = harvest ÷ hunters × 100, rounded to 0.1 (computed by us; ODWC prints counts only). Only single-animal permits are included: rows whose permit is a 2-deer permit (\"(2)\") are left out because harvest ÷ hunters there is deer per hunter, not the share of hunters who succeeded. Also left out: rows with no harvest printed (Tishomingo NWR gun; Great Salt Plains SP archery), rows with 0 hunters, rows ODWC prints as single permits whose matching draw hunts are 2-deer permits (Little River NWR gun 3160/3161, Pine Creek WMA youth 5130), turkey, and Special Family deer hunts (all 2-deer). huntCode is set only when exactly one ODWC hunt number in lib/huntdata/draw/ok.json has the same area, weapon, category and permit count (elk/antelope matched by area + permit type); where ODWC pooled several hunts at one area/method (e.g. Wichita Mountains NWR walk-in elk hunts 1030/1040/1050 and 1031/1041/1051, Cherokee GMA 3044+3046, Cookson WMA either-sex + antlerless), the row carries the area as unit and no huntCode. Permit counts in this harvest table do not always equal the draw tables (e.g. Wichita walk-in elk: 100 either-sex / 225 cow here vs 3 × 20 / 3 × 45 drawn — walk-in elk applications may cover up to 2 hunters), so treat the harvest permit text in label as ODWC's, not as tags. Statewide over-the-counter deer: ODWC publishes harvest by county (E-Check counts) and statewide success by method (Game Harvest Survey, chart only) in the 2025-26 Big Game Harvest Report, but no hunter numbers or success rate per county/zone, so no OTC rows. Same for OTC elk zones and archery pronghorn.",
